        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          After three launch delays in April caused by high winds at both the launch site and overseas emergency landing strips, Atlantis launched from Kennedy Space Center on the morning of May 19, 2000.  The STS-101 mission was originally designed to follow the launch of the Zvezda service module to outfit the Russian component as the early living quarters for the International Space Station (ISS) crews. When Zvezda's launch was delayed, Shuttle and Station managers agreed to fly Atlantis on two separate flights to the station: STS-101 to conduct maintenance and logistics work aboard the ISS in advance of Zvezda's arrival and STS-106 to unload supplies onto Zvezda from both the Shuttle and a Russian Progress resupply vehicle.  The STS-101 crew of seven included Mission Commander James D. Halsell, Pilot Scott J. "Doc" Horowitz, and Mission Specialists Mary Ellen Weber, Jeffrey N. Williams, James S. Voss, Susan J. Helms, and Yuri Usachev.
<P>
Atlantis arrived at the Space Station two days after launch and remained docked for six days, four of which were spent refurbishing and replacing components in both the Zarya and Unity modules. Two crewmembers performed a six and a half hour spacewalk the day after docking to install a Russian "Strela" cargo boom on the outside of Zarya.  They also replaced a faulty radio antenna associated with the early communications system on Unity, and performed several other tasks in advance of spacewalks on future station assembly missions.
<P>
The crew transferred almost one ton of equipment from a double Spacehab® module housed at the rear of Atlantis' cargo bay into the Zarya and Unity modules for use by the Expedition One crew. These logistical items included personal clothing and hygiene gear, medical and exercise equipment, computer equipment and printers, hardware for the eventual setup and activation of the station's Ku-band communications system, and a centerline camera for Unity's common berthing mechanisms to which other ISS components will be mated. Four large bags of water were also transferred from Atlantis to the Station for later use.
<P>
Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs) performed on this mission included "Monitoring Latent Virus Reactivation and Shedding in Astronauts (DSO 493) " and "Space Flight and Immune Function (DSO 498). "  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation that is performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers who serve as the test subjects.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power, and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics, and changes in the body's biochemistry.
<P>
Multiple Development Test Objectives (DTOs) were also performed on this mission. A DTO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers to evaluate new hardware and procedures involving the Orbiter, its subsystems, and its support equipment.
<P>
Other payloads flown on STS-101 included a Getaway Special Canister, a special container designed to carry small autonomous payloads.  The container transported the Mission to America's Remarkable Students (MARS) payload, which consisted of 20 tubes filled with materials chosen by schools from the United States and Canada.  Each school designed a payload that dealt specifically with space life sciences.  Among the experiment samples flown were various seeds, including watermelon, loquat, radish, green bean, weeping sand palm, daisy, soybean, turnip, and poppy.  Other materials placed in the tubes were freeze-dried bacteria, freeze-dried brine shrimp, plastic samples, soil, and yeast.
<P>
"Spuds in Space," an experiment developed by students from Shoshone-Bannock High School on the Fort Hall Indian Reservation in southeastern Idaho, also flew on STS-101.  This experiment compared the growth of potato plants in synthetic dirt, known as JSC Mars-1, on Earth with those that flew in space.  The simulated soil closely resembles the red dirt found on the surface of Mars.  The "Spuds in Space" experiment was the first test of the soil simulant as a medium for growing plants in space; it also marked the second time Native American students have flown an experiment on the Shuttle.
<P>
The Shuttle Atlantis landed at Kennedy Space Center on the evening of May 29, 2000.  This was the 14th night landing in Shuttle history.
